<section class="sp-sec"><h2>The setting</h2><p>By the autumn of 1949 the Communist armies had won the Chinese Civil War on the mainland, and Chiang Kai-shek's Nationalist government was retreating to Taiwan. Mao Zedong opened the Chinese People's Political Consultative Conference, convened in Beijing to found a new state, with a speech that cast the revolution as the end of a century of foreign humiliation.</p></section>
<section class="sp-sec"><h2>Key passage</h2><blockquote class="sp-quote"><p>The Chinese people, comprising one quarter of humanity, have now stood up.</p></blockquote><p class="sp-source">Opening address to the Chinese People's Political Consultative Conference, 21 September 1949. The words are often wrongly placed at the proclamation of the People's Republic on 1 October. Quoted briefly: the full text is still under copyright.</p><div class="sp-full"><button type="button" class="sp-share" data-share-text="“The Chinese people, comprising one quarter of humanity, have now stood up.” — Mao Zedong, Mao: The Chinese People Have Stood Up" data-share-img="/images/og/speech-mao-the-chinese-people-have-stood-up.jpg">Share this quote</button></div></section>
<section class="sp-sec"><h2>What followed</h2><p>On 1 October Mao proclaimed the People's Republic of China from Tiananmen in Beijing. The new state fought the United States in Korea and carried out a land reform that cost many lives, and under Mao's rule it went on to the Great Leap Forward, the famine that followed it, and the Cultural Revolution.</p><div class="sp-led"><h3 class="sp-led-k">What it led to</h3><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korean-war">Korean War</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-leap-forward">Great Leap Forward</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cultural-revolution">Cultural Revolution</a></div></div></section>
